<h2 id="start-with-the-work-people-need-to-finish">Start with the work people need to finish</h2>
<p>Mina opens her latest orders while a colleague runs a regional report and the shop continues accepting changes. Those operations need different budgets. A history page must feel responsive; a report may take longer; a change must complete its transaction before its caller can rely on the result.</p>
<p>Write the budget at the application boundary. For example, an invented service target might require 95% of history requests within 200 ms at 100 successful requests a second, with reports within 2 seconds and order changes within 300 ms. State the demand and mix under which those targets apply, an acceptable failure rate and resource headroom. A latency target without a workload says little about capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Latency</strong> measures how long one operation takes. <strong>Throughput</strong> counts completed operations per unit time. Keep successful useful completions separate from failures and timeouts. Record percentiles by operation and parameter class: p95 is a boundary at or below which 95% of the recorded latencies fall. An average can hide a painful slow tail.</p>
<p>Frequency changes priority. A 4 ms lookup performed 1,000 times a second accounts for four seconds of accumulated execution time each second; overlapping executions make that possible. A 2-second report once a minute contributes much less accumulated execution time, but may still disrupt every lookup while it runs. Neither sum establishes CPU consumption, because execution can include waiting.</p>
<p>Before changing anything, retain the statement shape and its frequency, typical and slow parameters, result sizes, table sizes and distributions, latency distributions, and concurrent activity over the same interval. Include the application’s connection wait, database round trips, result transfer and processing. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> explains how to make those boundaries visible.</p>

<h2 id="find-where-time-and-work-accumulate">Find where time and work accumulate</h2>
<p>A request that takes 160 ms does not identify a database problem. Figure 1 holds the total constant and changes where the delay lies. Switch cases and watch which measurement would direct the investigation.</p>
<PerformanceFigure scene="boundary" number={1} />
<p>When acquisition dominates, inspect connection ownership, admitted demand and the work already holding connections. When database execution dominates, inspect its work and waits. When transfer dominates, count returned bytes, rows and decoding work; a fast plan cannot make an unnecessarily huge response cheap. Repeated small queries can also accumulate round trips that no individual plan reveals.</p>
<p>Compare an ordinary execution with a slow one. Did the date range widen? Does one customer have far more orders? Was the same statement blocked by an unfinished transaction? Did a report start competing for CPU or storage? Put observations beside hypotheses: “more temporary blocks during reports” is evidence; “insufficient memory causes the delay” is an explanation to test.</p>
<p>For PostgreSQL, activity and wait samples, blocking relationships, interval changes in I/O counters and observed plans give different views of the same interval. A sampled wait can miss short events; a cumulative count needs a start and end and knowledge of resets. A high cache-hit ratio or busy processor alone does not identify wasted work. <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> owns monitoring and incident collection; here we follow one identified report into its execution.</p>

<h2 id="rows-have-a-physical-home-and-a-lifetime">Rows have a physical home and a lifetime</h2>
<p>A relational table is a logical set of rows. Storage gives those rows a physical home. Ordinary PostgreSQL tables use a <strong>heap</strong>: pages containing rows wherever space is available. Its usual page size is 8 KiB. Index entries lead to locations in that heap. Other engines may organise the table around a clustered key instead, so check the selected engine’s access path before counting its work.</p>
<p>A page holds several rows, and buffering happens in pages. Finding one row may require bringing its whole page into the engine’s cache. Nearby matches can share that work; scattered matches cannot. Figure 2 draws 36 invented rows, six per page. Compare four scattered matches with four nearby matches, then widen the request.</p>
<PerformanceFigure scene="pages" number={2} />
<p>The <strong>working set</strong> is the data the current workload repeatedly touches: relevant table and index pages, rather than simply the returned columns or the entire database. Wider inline rows and lower page occupancy can spread the same number of useful rows across more pages. Selecting fewer columns reduces transfer and may avoid fetching large out-of-line values, but does not repack the existing table pages.</p>
<p>A buffer hit avoids loading that page into the database cache, but still costs processing. A PostgreSQL buffer read can be served by the operating system’s file cache, so it is not proof of a physical device read. Repeated visits to a cached page can be expensive without producing any shared-buffer reads at all. Our report will demonstrate that case.</p>
<p>Rows also have a lifetime beyond their current value. PostgreSQL updates create row versions; cleanup cannot remove versions that a still-relevant snapshot may need. Old versions, index entries and reusable gaps can make the physical footprint larger than the visible row count suggests. Ordinary vacuum makes eligible space reusable and usually leaves most of the file allocated. A long snapshot can therefore change later queries’ working conditions while returning no extra rows itself.</p>
<p>After completing this article, choose <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">How stored rows become physical work</a> when unexplained page traffic, working-set growth or retained space makes that physical organisation consequential. It investigates width, locality, cleanup and pruning; transaction guarantees remain with <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a>.</p>

<h2 id="give-the-query-a-useful-route-to-its-rows">Give the query a useful route to its rows</h2>
<p>Mina’s history asks for ten orders, newest first:</p>
<Sql code={lookup} label="A bounded customer history" />
<p>A scan can examine every order, reject other customers and old dates, then sort the survivors. A B-tree index on <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code> offers another route: locate customer 42’s date range, read in the requested order and stop after ten matches. A composite key sorts by its first column, then by the next within equal first values. Here equality fixes the customer before date ordering matters; the order identifier breaks ties.</p>
<p><strong>Selectivity</strong> is the fraction matching a condition. A selective request can avoid most table work through an index. A broad request may reach most pages anyway and pay index work as well. On a tiny table, scanning a few pages can be cheaper than a search. On a larger one, scattered base-row fetches can make an apparently selective route costly. The planner estimates these alternatives rather than treating index use as a goal.</p>
<p>An index must also be eligible for the actual predicate and ordering. A function or conversion around a column may need a matching expression index or a carefully verified predicate rewrite. Parameter types, collation and optional conditions matter. PostgreSQL 18 has some additional skip-scan opportunities, but they do not make every composite key equally useful.</p>
<p>A <strong>covering index</strong> contains every column the query needs. PostgreSQL can store returned values with <code>INCLUDE</code>, but avoiding heap visits also depends on page visibility information: changing pages can make an index-only scan visit the table. Check observed heap fetches rather than assuming coverage eliminates them.</p>
<p>Every candidate index also takes space and work to build and maintain. Inserts add entries; updates can change index work and the opportunities for cheap row-version updates. Evaluate the index set against lookups, joins, changes and maintenance together. After finishing the comparison method below, choose <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes for a workload</a> when the evidence calls for designing or comparing routes. It supplies its own compact plan legend.</p>

<h2 id="explain-the-work-and-test-a-plan-hypothesis">Explain the work and test a plan hypothesis</h2>
<p>The shop’s report ranks north-region customers by spending. It returns only ten rows, but every qualifying order contributes to deciding those ten.</p>
<Sql code={report} label="The report used throughout this investigation" />
<p>The disposable verification fixture contains 10,000 customers and 100,000 orders, ten per customer. Orders have a primary-key index and an index on <code>customer_id</code>. We collect statistics with only ten north customers, then change another 7,990 to north. Automatic analysis is disabled only on this fixture’s customer table while demonstrating stale statistics, and restored afterwards. In a service, establish whether statistics actually lag before choosing that explanation.</p>
<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows a chosen plan and predictions. <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)</code> executes it and records rows and buffers, plus total execution time. Collect execution evidence on disposable data first: effects, functions, triggers, locks and resource consumption still happen. Wrapping a command in rollback does not undo every possible effect, including sequence advances or external work.</p>
<p>Read the reporting units before interpreting the evidence:</p>
<ul>
 <li><code>cost=startup..total</code> gives planner units, not milliseconds. The first number prices work before the first row; the second assumes full consumption. <code>width</code> predicts average output bytes per row.</li>
 <li>Estimated <code>rows</code> predicts rows emitted by a node, rather than every row it examines. Observed rows report the execution; repeated nodes report averages per loop. Multiply by loops to understand total emitted row work, allowing for displayed rounding.</li>
 <li>When node timing is enabled, <code>actual time</code> is milliseconds per loop and includes child work. Do not add inclusive node times. Our capture disables that timing to reduce instrumentation; it cannot identify exclusive time in a node.</li>
 <li>Buffer figures also include child work. Do not sum the tree. Hits and reads are accesses, not a count of distinct pages. Temporary blocks describe intermediate work; they are separate from table and index buffers.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is the before plan from PostgreSQL 18.6, shortened and relabelled to align the estimates and observations. Costs, widths and buffer lines are omitted here; the figure gives the captured top-level buffers.</p>
<pre class="a05-plan"><code>{plan}</code></pre>
<p>Follow rows from the indented inputs towards their consumers. The customer scan filters to 8,000 rows. The nested loop asks the order index for each customer, producing ten orders per probe: <code>10 × 8,000 = 80,000</code> joined rows. The following sort orders them by customer so group aggregation can sum each group. A final top-N sort ranks the 8,000 totals and returns ten. The lower sort must process all 80,000 inputs even though the final result is small.</p>
<p>The consequential surprise appears early: 18 estimated north customers versus 8,000 observed. The order probe itself correctly predicts ten rows per customer; it is repeated far more often than the plan expected. The lower sort uses an external merge with 1,496 kB of disk work reported. A spill is evidence of intermediate work exceeding the available in-memory route, not proof that raising a global memory setting is the remedy.</p>
<p>Known distribution drift supports a specific hypothesis: old customer statistics make repeated probes look cheap. Predict that <code>ANALYZE customers;</code> will improve that estimate and may replace repeated order lookups and the spilling group-input sort. Figure 3 walks from prediction to observed work, then shows the targeted refresh.</p>
<PerformanceFigure scene="plan" number={3} />
<p>After the refresh, the customer estimate is 8,000. PostgreSQL chooses a <strong>hash join</strong>: build a lookup structure for the qualifying customers, scan orders once and probe it. Hash aggregation forms the 8,000 totals without the previous input sort; the final top-N ordering remains. Another available method, a <strong>merge join</strong>, advances through inputs ordered on their join keys. These are ways to implement the same join, with different setup, ordering, repeated-work and memory costs.</p>
<p>The captured top-level shared hits fell from 96,104 to 745; temporary reads fell from 187 blocks to zero. This supports the predicted removal of work even though both plans found their table and index pages in shared buffers. The after plan’s estimated total cost was higher than the before plan’s, because the old prediction had priced a much smaller job. Comparing costs across changed statistics is not measuring a speed-up.</p>
<p>Fresh statistics still approximate distributions. Accurate estimates can correctly predict an expensive report, and skewed parameters or the application’s prepared execution path can select a different plan from a literal-only check. The final section completes this ordinary remedy’s comparison here. Afterwards, choose <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a> for deeper operator tracing, estimate errors and common versus rare prepared parameters; that destination completes its own investigation.</p>

<h2 id="a-faster-query-can-still-make-the-workload-slower">A faster query can still make the workload slower</h2>
<p>The report shares a service with short reads and changes. More active executions can compete for CPU, storage bandwidth, buffers, temporary space and locks. Parallel workers may reduce one report’s elapsed time while consuming resources that several short requests need.</p>
<p>Memory limits are especially easy to misread. PostgreSQL’s <code>work_mem</code> applies to individual sort and hash operations, with additional rules for hashes; a query can contain several, and concurrent sessions and parallel workers multiply potential demand. Raising it globally after seeing one spill can exchange temporary work for memory pressure across the service.</p>
<p>A hot order row can serialise changes while most processors remain idle. A report holding an old snapshot can also retain versions and enlarge future work. Diagnose the contested resource and transaction lifetime before adding connections or weakening guarantees. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Transaction design</a> owns lock and retry correctness; <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">application integration</a> owns connection lifetimes and admission.</p>
<p>Figure 4 makes a deliberately narrow point. One serial bottleneck completes one job per tick. Limit admission, then remove work. Compare the last caller’s completion time across the application and database together.</p>
<PerformanceFigure scene="load" number={4} />
<p>Moving a queue is not itself faster completion. In a real system, bounded active work can also reduce competition and improve the completion rate, but that effect needs measurement. After finishing this article, choose <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Improving performance under concurrent load</a> when isolated execution cannot explain tail latency or completed throughput. It follows arrivals, outstanding work, resource competition and admission through a mixed workload.</p>

<h2 id="change-one-explanation-into-a-measured-improvement">Change one explanation into a measured improvement</h2>
<p>Close the report investigation by comparing useful work, not just the new estimate. In our fixture, both executions returned the same ordered customer identifiers and exact totals. A separate five-customer fixture checked multiple orders, tied totals, the inclusive start date and exclusion of the other region, an earlier order and a customer with no orders. It returned customers 2 then 3 at 20.00 each; the declared non-null total rejected a null insert. A rewrite must preserve those meanings before its performance matters.</p>
<p>We repeated three short mixed-workload runs before and after <code>ANALYZE customers</code>. Three pgbench clients each completed 200 transactions using the same scripts and random seed. Each run contained 364 customer lookups, 176 revision-counter updates and 60 reports. Clients waited for completion before issuing more work; their reported latency includes the local client/database exchange but no HTTP handler or connection-pool wait. Figure 5 keeps each class visible.</p>
<PerformanceFigure scene="comparison" number={5} />
<p>Report p95 fell from 55.12–56.61 ms to 15.73–16.30 ms across the three runs. Lookup and change p95 ranges remained similar, while total completion rate increased. Together with the removed probes and spill, this supports accepting the statistics refresh for this fixture. The hypothesis survived its test; the query and index definitions did not change.</p>
<p>These are warm, short observations on a bounded shared PostgreSQL 18.6 instance: 32 MB shared buffers, 4 MB work memory and no parallel workers. They establish neither production capacity nor an arrival rate the service can sustain. Closed-loop clients reduce offered demand when responses slow, the runs are too short to cover maintenance cycles, and the revision updates do not model order fulfilment. No CPU, device-I/O or WAL-rate comparison was collected. Keep that uncertainty alongside the result.</p>
<p>Use the following method for a change you intend to deploy:</p>
<ol>
 <li><strong>State the prediction and acceptance rules first.</strong> Name the work expected to disappear, the measurements that should change and the cost that may rise. For this report: fewer repeated probes, lower report tail latency, no result changes and no unacceptable short-request regression.</li>
 <li><strong>Hold meaning and conditions steady.</strong> Verify results and edge cases. Retain engine and driver versions, relevant settings, schema, data generator, size, skew, query mix, transaction lengths, parameters and preparation path. Change one explanation at a time.</li>
 <li><strong>Exercise representative conditions.</strong> Include common and rare parameter classes, warm-up and sustained competition, ongoing changes and maintenance. Repeat comparable runs to expose variance. Test a cold-start condition separately on a disposable instance; restarting does not necessarily clear the operating system’s cache.</li>
 <li><strong>Measure at the user’s boundary.</strong> Record end-to-end latency distributions by class, offered and admitted demand, useful completions, failures and remaining work. Correlate CPU, storage and temporary work, log generation, memory and blocking over that same interval. A queue that keeps growing is not a sustainable operating point.</li>
 <li><strong>Accept the whole cost.</strong> Include index size and write overhead, statistics collection, maintenance duration and rollout risk. Retain the before/after evidence, a stop condition for the experiment, rollback or recovery arrangements and a regression threshold for the deployed workload.</li>
</ol>
<p>Match the intervention to the explanation rather than trying every setting. The alternatives make different predictions:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Match a remedy to evidence"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Candidate change</th><th>Cost or condition to test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Unnecessary repeated queries or multiplied intermediate rows</td><td>Rewrite or bound the requested work</td><td>Result equivalence, round trips, response size and lock duration</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Selective history reads scan and sort large inputs</td><td>A workload-shaped index</td><td>Base-row fetches, write overhead, storage and build impact</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Estimate drift after a distribution change</td><td>Targeted statistics refresh, as above</td><td>Changed estimates and actual work; continuing analysis cost</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Excess pages or retained versions explain growing work</td><td>Allow cleanup, or test a physical-layout change</td><td>Reuse versus shrinkage, rewrite traffic, locks and recurrence</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Necessary work saturates an identified resource</td><td>Reduce competition or add that resource</td><td>Whole-workload completion, queueing and the next limiting resource</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The refresh itself reads data and consumes resources; it also requires a policy that keeps statistics useful as the shop changes. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> owns the rollout gates for successful changes, with schema-operation details in its contextual guidance. Do not turn a successful experiment into an unbounded production maintenance command.</p>
<p>Mina’s history, the report and order changes now have a repeatable path from symptom to decision: locate the delay, explain the physical work, predict a targeted change, verify the answer and compare the mixed workload. Growth can reopen the investigation. More orders, a newly dominant region or longer transactions can change its conditions even when the SQL stays identical. Use the focused investigations below when the next observation makes their question consequential.</p>

